From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Paiva <felipe.paiva@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 22:30:00 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] lynx-core-tests

Beetle Lynx (Atari Lynx) runs on the generic libretro host before any
Lynx platform code exists. With no boot ROM a BS93 homebrew image runs
from RAM and paints the 160x102 picture in the middle of the 240x160
buffer; its state round-trips and the core exposes no save RAM.

The core reports 75 fps but runs one Lynx display frame per retro_run:
the boot ROM and the homebrew preset leave the timers at 59.9 Hz (736
samples a frame at 44.1 kHz), a game that sets 75 Hz gets 588, and
lynx_force_60hz changes only the reported number.

With no lynxboot.img, or one under 512 bytes, a .lnx cart loads with no
error and never draws: every frame is the core's undrawn fill and runs
to its 700000-cycle cap. With SLOT_LYNX_BIOS pointing at the real boot
ROM (512 bytes, crc32 0d973c9d) a synthetic cart boots through the cc65
encrypted loader, draws, round-trips its state, and shows that the core
turns the pad for a rotated cart only when lynx_rot_screen asks it to.
Without the variable those tests print a BIOS skip.

The encrypted one-block loader is cc65's libsrc/lynx/bootldr.s by Karri
Kaksonen (zlib licence). The test approach follows nelynes/slotPlus's
Lynx work, commit 749523c (https://github.com/nelynes/slotPlus/commit/749523c,
GPL-3.0); the carts and checks are new.
